__MATH_530__

Thus, the moment of inertia of the pendulum depends on both the mass __MATH_531__ of a body and its geometry, or shape, as defined by the distance __MATH_532__ to the axis of rotation.

This simple formula generalizes to define moment of inertia for an arbitrarily shaped body as the sum of all the elemental point masses __MATH_533__ each multiplied by the square of its perpendicular distance __MATH_534__ to an axis __MATH_535__. An arbitrary object's moment of inertia thus depends on the spatial distribution of its mass.

