=== Compound pendulums ===

A [[compound pendulum]] is a body formed from an assembly of particles of continuous shape that rotates rigidly around a pivot. Its moment of inertia is the sum of the moments of inertia of each of the particles that it is composed of. The [[resonance|natural]] [[angular frequency|frequency]] (__MATH_561__) of a compound pendulum depends on its moment of inertia, __MATH_562__,
__MATH_563__
where __MATH_564__ is the mass of the object, __MATH_565__ is local acceleration of gravity, and __MATH_566__ is the distance from the pivot point to the center of mass of the object. Measuring this frequency of oscillation over small angular displacements provides an effective way of measuring moment of inertia of a body.

Thus, to determine the moment of inertia of the body, simply suspend it from a convenient pivot point __MATH_567__ so that it swings freely in a plane perpendicular to the direction of the desired moment of inertia, then measure its natural frequency or period of oscillation (__MATH_568__), to obtain
__MATH_569__
where __MATH_570__ is the period (duration) of oscillation (usually averaged over multiple periods).

==== Center of oscillation ====
A simple pendulum that has the same natural frequency as a compound pendulum defines the length __MATH_571__ from the pivot to a point called the [[center of oscillation]] of the compound pendulum. This point also corresponds to the [[center of percussion]]. The length __MATH_572__ is determined from the formula,
