It is common in rigid body mechanics to use notation that explicitly identifies the __MATH_830__, __MATH_831__, and __MATH_832__-axes, such as __MATH_833__ and __MATH_834__, for the components of the inertia tensor.

=== Alternate inertia convention ===
There are some CAD and CAE applications such as SolidWorks, Unigraphics NX/Siemens NX and MSC Adams that use an alternate convention for the products of inertia. According to this convention, the minus sign is removed from the product of inertia formulas and instead inserted in the inertia matrix:
__MATH_835__

==== Determine inertia convention (principal axes method) ====
If one has the inertia data __MATH_836__ without knowing which inertia convention that has been used, it can be determined if one also has the [[#Principal axes|principal axes]]. With the principal axes method, one makes inertia matrices from the following two assumptions:

# The standard inertia convention has been used __MATH_837__.  
# The alternate inertia convention has been used __MATH_838__. 
Next, one calculates the eigenvectors for the two matrices. The matrix whose eigenvectors are parallel to the principal axes corresponds to the inertia convention that has been used.

